---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/privy-vs-brevo';
const title = 'Privy vs Brevo for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Privy and Brevo for Shopify capture, email, SMS, connector scope, campaign controls and contribution economics.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can both products send email and SMS?",
    "answer": "Yes. Privy markets email, SMS and capture tools. Brevo offers email, SMS and transactional messaging. Confirm contracted products, account access, sender registration and recipient countries before selecting either."
  },
  {
    "question": "When might Privy fit?",
    "answer": "Privy is worth testing when a Shopify team wants capture, email and SMS together and its required segments, exits and supported markets work in the account pilot."
  },
  {
    "question": "When might Brevo fit?",
    "answer": "Brevo is worth testing when a team wants email and SMS campaigns with Shopify data, provided the selected connector, plan, markets and account controls pass the pilot."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should another email or SMS tool be added?",
    "answer": "Only to close a demonstrated gap. Assign one owner per message job, prove suppression and measure the additional cost before introducing another sender."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should capture feed automation?",
    "answer": "Keep permission evidence separate by channel, map stable customer identifiers, and test guest checkout and profile merges. An email subscription or phone number alone does not establish SMS permission."
  },
  {
    "question": "How do purchase exits need testing?",
    "answer": "Place a test order while a message is queued. The responsible workflow must recheck order truth, retire the pending promotion and avoid duplicate sends when events arrive late or are retried."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does software guarantee consent compliance?",
    "answer": "No. Verify applicable recipient rules, disclosure, permission evidence, opt-out handling and quiet hours with qualified review. A vendor feature does not establish permission for a particular campaign."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should costs be compared?",
    "answer": "Use matched eligible groups and a common measurement window. Compare incremental contribution after net sales, merchandise and variable costs, messaging, allocated subscriptions and operating effort; count discounts and returns once."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are the merchant examples measured results?",
    "answer": "No. All fourteen are fictional proposed acceptance fixtures. Their thresholds are test inputs, not customer results, product guarantees or a ranking."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-14 md:py-20">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Privy vs Brevo</nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl">
      <p class="badge">Shopify campaign comparison</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Privy vs Brevo for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Test the campaign you need to operate. Privy combines capture, email and SMS; Brevo offers email, SMS and transactional messaging, with Shopify connectivity and account scope to verify. The useful choice is the one that passes your audience, order and delivery controls at an acceptable cost.</p>
    </header>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Current scope and conditional fit</h2>
      <p>Reviewed November 25, 2026. <a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/">Privy</a> describes popups, targeted capture, email and SMS campaigns and automated journeys. Its <a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/pricing">pricing page</a> separates email, an SMS add-on and a displays-only option. Test Privy when a Shopify team wants those jobs together; verify the exact automation steps, audience conditions and account access before treating a template as a complete solution.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360014912879-Brevo-PushOwl-app-for-Shopify-Connect-your-Shopify-store-with-Brevo-PushOwl">Brevo’s Shopify documentation</a> now identifies Brevo PushOwl as the supported connection for Shopify, replacing the former app for new stores. It describes email and SMS campaigns and synchronization of customers, products and orders. Brevo is a positive candidate when those connected commerce journeys meet the team's needs. Verify the installed connector version and chosen account before assuming older setup instructions still apply. The documentation warns that a Shopify import may overwrite contact attributes, including subscription status: reconcile channel permissions and suppression history before enabling sends.</p>
      <p>Brevo also documents <a class="link" href="https://developers.brevo.com/docs/transactional-sms-endpoints">transactional SMS</a> for non-promotional notifications and confirmations. Keep service notifications and promotional campaigns separately owned; a transaction or API label does not authorize marketing. Confirm which Shopify notifications remain with Shopify, which move to Brevo, and prove that an order produces only the intended confirmation. Transactional API capability alone does not establish that the Shopify connector configures this automatically.</p>
      <p>Confirm both vendors' account eligibility, sender registration, supported sending and recipient countries, reply handling and plan-specific automation access. Request current scope and usage quotes from <a class="link" href="https://www.brevo.com/pricing/">Brevo</a> and Privy, including any separate connector subscription, marketing and transactional allowances, SMS credits and overages. Do not assume a general Brevo plan includes every PushOwl feature or identical billing. No fixed price or universal geographic availability is assumed here.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Privy candidate</th><th>Brevo candidate</th><th>Acceptance evidence</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Capture to welcome</td><td>Displays plus email/SMS journeys</td><td>Email/SMS with verified connector capture inputs</td><td>Channel permission and stable identity survive the handoff</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shopify event journeys</td><td>Test required ecommerce conditions in the chosen account</td><td>Test PushOwl sync and plan-specific journeys</td><td>Order, refund and delayed-event fixtures pass</td></tr>
            <tr><td>SMS calendar</td><td>Confirm market and account support</td><td>Confirm plan, region, registration and sending mode</td><td>Eligible audience, queue cancellation and reply handling demonstrated</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operating cost</td><td>Quote contacts, displays and SMS mix</td><td>Quote plan, connector, transactional usage and SMS mix</td><td>Matched contribution worksheet and operator time</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h2>Fourteen fictional proposed acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>These merchant names and scenarios are fictional test proposals, not testimonials or measured performance. Numbers below are hypothetical inputs. For each job, Privy may fit if its capture and journeys pass the test in the selected account; Brevo may fit if its email/SMS journeys and mapped Shopify data pass the same test within the selected plan and connector scope. Neither receives a pass from a feature list. Record the configured path, evidence, owner and failure response for each fixture.</p>
      <h3>Festival Threads Co: City-specific tour routing</h3>
      <p>Select only the opted-in city audience; schedule by recipient timezone rather than assuming the venue timezone. Propose a maximum of three texts per city and reject a fourth across overlapping tours.</p>
      <h3>Sauce Lab: Batch scarcity</h3>
      <p>Propose one launch and one scarcity message. A 200-bottle threshold is a fictional input: require fresh sellable inventory and the correct batch before release; cancel a stale queued alert.</p>
      <h3>Ember Ceramics: Kiln waitlist fairness</h3>
      <p>Use a fictional 4,200-person waitlist for 80 pieces. Preserve priority timestamps and fulfillment order; a retry must not move a shopper ahead or promise inventory already allocated.</p>
      <h3>Ridge Run Coffee: Subscription exclusions</h3>
      <p>Exclude active subscribers from acquisition flash offers using authoritative subscription status. A delayed subscription update must stop the queued promotion before delivery.</p>
      <h3>Cipher Audio: Full-price launch sequence</h3>
      <p>Propose education two weeks before launch and a launch text only for eligible engaged browsers. Keep the offer at full price and test that a purchase after the teaser exits the launch reminder.</p>
      <h3>Patchwork Denim: One-of-one style targeting</h3>
      <p>Use volunteered style preferences for new listings. Suppress an item immediately when sold; two shoppers viewing a single piece must not produce two availability promises.</p>
      <h3>Arena Energy: Athlete collaboration</h3>
      <p>Target an opted-in gym-interest audience without inferring sensitive health data. An email-only shaker offer must not leak into SMS copy; approve both versions before scheduling.</p>
      <h3>Loom & Frame: Edition sell-through</h3>
      <p>Propose an alert at half of a disclosed edition sold to eligible three-order collectors. Reconcile cancellations and returns before evaluating the threshold; reject a duplicate crossing event.</p>
      <h3>Driftwood Surf Co: Seasonal restock</h3>
      <p>Coordinate swell education with a product-specific waitlist restock notice. Forecast content does not prove stock; verify variant availability and retire an obsolete seasonal queue.</p>
      <h3>Noir Collective: VIP drop access</h3>
      <p>Propose a Thursday drop with a two-hour VIP window. Verify access entitlement and timezone, then test a two-per-week cross-channel promotional cap as a fictional policy.</p>
      <h3>SoleDrop ATX: Size-run priority</h3>
      <p>Route sneaker access by size and waitlist position. A fictional 70-percent sell-through threshold must not trigger general access if the recipient size is gone; expired links must fail safely.</p>
      <h3>Vinyl Vault Records: Collector value</h3>
      <p>Propose first access for waitlisted collectors with two orders or a hypothetical $400 net-value threshold. Reconcile returns, currency and identity before assigning the segment; thresholds confer no SMS permission.</p>
      <h3>Bloom & Wick: Gifting calendar</h3>
      <p>Propose an August planning exercise separating gift intent from self-purchase. Verify destination-specific shipping deadlines and bundle stock; remove the deadline alert after the cutoff.</p>
      <h3>Peak Trail Gear: Clearance restraint</h3>
      <p>Offer a hypothetical 15-percent clearance incentive only to eligible bargain-sensitive customers. Keep full-price loyalists and the holdout out of the queue; approve the contribution model before release.</p>
      <h2>Permission, identity and order truth</h2>
      <p>Retain channel-specific permission source, timestamp, disclosure version, scope and revocation history. Quarantine records with missing proof; importing a phone number or email subscription must not enroll it in SMS. Normalize phone numbers and map Shopify customer and order identifiers; test guest checkout, changed addresses, merged profiles and shared numbers without transferring permission between people.</p>
      <p>Use Shopify orders and inventory as authoritative commerce records, with defined handling for cancellations, refunds and subscription changes. Recheck eligibility immediately before dispatch. A purchase must exit the relevant cart or launch reminder across every sender, including already queued work. Test a purchase during a delay, an out-of-order update and a cancellation after scheduling. Missing or stale truth should pause the affected job, not create an urgency claim.</p>
      <h2>Queue control, approvals and handoff</h2>
      <p>Assign one owner to each message job and a shared promotional calendar. Test duplicate event delivery and timeouts using a durable key combining customer, job, event and campaign version; retry only unconfirmed work after checking provider status. A transport retry must not create a second message. If the chosen tool cannot prove these controls, narrow the workflow or add an explicitly owned integration and retest.</p>
      <p>Require approval of audience, permission, offer, inventory claim, links, timezone policy and final copy before scheduling. Test opt-out and help replies with supported sender types. Apply recipient-specific quiet hours and frequency policies after qualified review of applicable rules; no universal legal sending window is assumed here. Keep email and SMS preferences distinct while honoring a request's actual scope and blocking ambiguous permissions for review.</p>
      <p>For a handoff, name the outgoing and incoming owners, record a cutover time, reconcile permissions and suppressions, and retire old queued campaigns and delayed flows. Prove the old sender is silent before enabling the new one. Rollback starts by pausing the new queue, reconciling sends and purchases, and restoring only approved unsent work under one owner; never replay a historical queue wholesale. These are proposed operator acceptance requirements, not claims that either product provides every control automatically.</p>
      <h2>Matched hypothetical contribution economics</h2>
      <p>Use randomly assigned eligible groups, a shared observation window and deduplicated order truth. Compare campaign and holdout contribution per eligible customer, then scale the difference to the same population and subtract incremental messaging, allocated platform fees and operating effort. Platform-attributed sales alone do not establish incrementality.</p>
      <p>Fictional arithmetic: each group has 1,000 eligible customers. Campaign net sales of $12,000 less $6,000 merchandise cost and $1,000 variable fulfillment/payment costs produce $5,000 contribution. Holdout net sales of $10,000 less $5,000 merchandise cost and $900 variable costs produce $4,100. The matched difference is $900, or $0.90 per eligible customer. Subtract hypothetical $150 messaging, $200 allocated software and $100 operating effort: $450 remains, or $0.45 per eligible customer. These are inputs, not observed returns or vendor prices. Net sales already include discounts and returns; do not subtract either again. Include return timing, migration effort and uncertainty before judging scale.</p>
      <p>Request current quotes for identical contacts, sends, SMS encoding/segments, recipient countries, add-ons, credit expiry or rollover and peak volume. Read <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> for planning context. Do not add Sequenzy, Postscript or another sender unless a demonstrated gap justifies its coordination and cost. Either candidate can be evaluated as the owner of supported email and SMS jobs.</p>
      <h2>Decision after the pilot</h2>
      <p>Choose Privy if the proposed capture and campaign path passes your fixtures with manageable operating effort. Choose Brevo if its coordinated email/SMS journeys pass the same controls and the selected plan and connector scope fits your markets and operating team. If neither passes, document the failed gate and revise the scope before sending. No merchant result, fixed price, universal winner or forced stack is asserted.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy vs Brevo FAQ" />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
